Selection Sort in JavaLast Updated : 17 Mar 2025Selection sort is an easy-to-understand and straightforward kind of algorithm that sorts elements in a list or array, either ascending or descending. The element idea at the core of selection sort is to obtain repeatedly the minimum (or the maximum) element of the unsorted part and swap it with the element at the beginning of the unsorted part. This process of sorting will continue until the entire inventory is sorted.Here's a brief overview of the selection sort algorithm:Selection: The algorithm iterates through the unprocessed array, looking for the minimum (or maximum) element.Swap: After finding the minimum (or maximum) element, it is then swapped with the element that is at the front of the remaining unsorted portion. This operation consequently lets the element appear at its correct sorted place.Shrink: The sorted part is enlarged with each next iteration, but the unsorted portion shrinks. The sorting continues until the whole collection will be sorted.The selection sort is not the most efficient sorting method; nevertheless, even for large datasets, it has a time complexity of O(n^2) in all cases. Nevertheless, it is generally used for teaching purposes because of its simplicity and ability for instant implementation. Moreover, this in-place approach makes it an ideal candidate where memory usage is an issue.How does selection sort work?The selection sort algorithm works in a very simple way. It maintains two subarray for the given array.The subarray is already sorted.And the second subarray is unsorted.With every iteration of selection sort, an element is picked from the unsorted subarray and moved to the sorted subarray.Let's walk through the selection sort process step by step for the example array [5, 1, 12, -5, 16, 2, 12, 14].Initial Array: [5, 1, 12, -5, 16, 2, 12, 14]Iteration 1:Find the minimum element in the entire array. In this case, -5 is the smallest element at index 3.Swap -5 with the first element 5.Updated array: [-5, 1, 12, 5, 16, 2, 12, 14]Iteration 2:Find the minimum element in the subarray [1, 12, 5, 16, 2, 12, 14]. The smallest element is 1 at index 1.Swap 1 with the second element 5.Updated array: [-5, 1, 12, 5, 16, 2, 12, 14]Iteration 3:Find the minimum element in the subarray [12, 5, 16, 2, 12, 14]. The smallest element is 2 at index 5.Swap 2 with the third element 12.Updated array: [-5, 1, 2, 5, 16, 12, 12, 14]Iteration 4:Find the minimum element in the subarray [5, 16, 12, 12, 14]. The smallest element is 5 at index 3.Swap 5 with the fourth element 16.Updated array: [-5, 1, 2, 5, 16, 12, 12, 14]Iteration 5:Find the minimum element in the subarray [16, 12, 12, 14]. The smallest element is 12 at index 5.Swap 12 with the fifth element 16.Updated array: [-5, 1, 2, 5, 12, 16, 12, 14]Iteration 6:Find the minimum element in the subarray [16, 12, 14]. The smallest element is 12 at index 6.Swap 12 with the sixth element 16.Updated array: [-5, 1, 2, 5, 12, 12, 16, 14]Iteration 7:Find the minimum element in the subarray [16, 14]. The smallest element is 14 at index 7.Swap 14 with the seventh element 16.Updated array: [-5, 1, 2, 5, 12, 12, 14, 16]Final Sorted Array: [-5, 1, 2, 5, 12, 12, 14, 16]After seven iterations, the array is completely sorted in ascending order.Let's represent each iteration of the selection sort algorithm with a diagram:Selection Sort Implementation:SelectionSort.javapublic class SelectionSort {
    // Function to perform selection sort on an array
    public static void selectionSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;

        // Iterate through the array
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            // Find the index of the minimum element in the unsorted portion of the array
            int minIndex = i;
            // Search for the minimum element in the unsorted portion
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                    minIndex = j;
                }
            }
            // Swap the found minimum element with the first element of the unsorted portion
            int temp = arr[minIndex];
            arr[minIndex] = arr[i];
            arr[i] = temp;
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example array
        int[] arr = {5, 1, 12, -5, 16, 2, 12, 14};       
        System.out.println("Before Selection sort:");
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
        // Sort the array using the selection sort
        selectionSort(arr);
        // Print the sorted array
        System.out.println("After Selection sort:");
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
Output:Before Selection sort:
5 1 12 -5 16 2 12 14 
After Selection sort:
-5 1 2 5 12 12 14 16
Time Complexity:The time complexity of selection sort is always O(n^2), where 'n' is the number of elements in the array. This is the case because of the two loops that are nested in the selection sort. Each time the outer loop runs 'n' times, the inner loop runs (n-1), (n-2), (n-3), ..., 1 respectively. This leads to a sum of n*(n-1)/2 swaps and comparisons.Space Complexity:Selection sort is an in-place sorting algorithm that sorts an array without the requirement of additional space, which is only a few variables for iteration. The space complexity of the selection sort is O(n), which implies that the space occupied by the algorithm does not vary depending on the size of the input array.Advantages of Selection Sort AlgorithmSimple Implementation: Selection sort stands out for its simplicity and ease of understanding. It is performed by looking through the array numerous times, finding the smallest (or biggest) member and then exchanging it with the first unsorted item. This simplicity makes it applicable not only for educational purposes but also as a starting base for learning sorting algorithms.In-Place Sorting: The selection sort uses an in-place sorting strategy, which does not depend on additional memory proportional to the size of the array other than a few iteration variables. This attribute makes it memory efficient, which is especially critical when sorting large arrays, where memory usage is an issue.Stable Sorting: Among all the sorting algorithms, selection sort is a stable one in the sense that it retains the initial order of equal elements of the sorted array. If there is more than one element with the same values, the original order is preserved while sorting.Adaptive: The selection sort algorithm, regardless of its n^2 time complexity in all cases, can be fast enough if the array size is small or there are not many inversions. In these cases, the number of comparisons and swaps is decreased, thus leading to better efficiency compared to other O(n^2) algorithms.Useful for Small Lists or Nearly Sorted Lists: The selection sort will be effective in sorting short lists or lists that are almost in the sorted order. While it performs the same number of swaps for each element, it can run faster than more complicated algorithms for inputs of very small size or inputs with most elements already close to the sorted positions.Disadvantages of Selection Sort AlgorithmQuadratic Time Complexity: The time complexity of the selection sort is O(n^2) for an array with 'n' elements. Accordingly, with the growing size of the array, the sorting time becomes proportional to the square of the array. Where selection sort is inadequate for large datasets, mergesort or quicksort, which have, respectively, better average and worst-case time complexities, will be practical.Lack of Adaptiveness: Selection sort doesn't keep up with the data. It does never perform the same number of comparisons and swaps no matter whether the input array is already partially sorted or not. This is the reason why it is unsuitable for situations where the input data might already be partially sorted because it does not eliminate necessary operations.Inefficient for Large Datasets: However, selection sort, which has quadratic time complexity, does not make sense for sorting large data sets. With the growth in the size of the input array, the efficiency of selection sort begins to worsen significantly, which makes it less competent than more efficient algorithms like quicksort or heapsort for solving real-life problems.Large Number of Swaps: Selection sort does lots of swapping even if the array is already somewhat arranged. This leads to slower speed in accessing memory pattern patterns, particularly when processing arrays and arrays that are stored in slow memory devices.Not Suitable for Linked Lists: Although selection sort can be realized for arrays, it is not efficient for linked lists. This is the reason why, in the case of linked lists, the sequential access of elements won't work well, so arrays become the better choice.Next TopicJava Programs ← prev next →
